{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1083 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "852b76bcc4001399725c2eb894741709aca545a0cebc2cd5746d8237687a36fd" :edb/db-before-t 1082 :edb/db-after-t 1083 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534395 50 #inst "2026-10-07T00:03:50.110Z" 13194139534395 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286102 13194139534395 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286116 13194139534395 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286109 13194139534395 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286123 13194139534395 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286130 13194139534395 true] [17592186286102 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286103 13194139534395 true] [17592186286102 1089 "q-12124" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286102 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534395 true] [17592186286102 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,1)$\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286102 1113 #uuid "18446b37-20cc-5e44-a427-24676c932277" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286102 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286104 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286105 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286106 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286107 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286108 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1041 "selection" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1104 #edb/ref 17592186286104 13194139534395 true] [17592186286103 1110 #uuid "7765a4c1-9acf-5c4a-b959-40fe56dae2b9" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286104 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534395 true] [17592186286104 1111 #uuid "911977f9-4dd7-5952-a7d2-5e7840939b58" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286105 1034 "I only" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534395 true] [17592186286105 1111 #uuid "ffb95726-da34-5373-afcd-2fe81b267f91" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286106 1034 "III only" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534395 true] [17592186286106 1111 #uuid "f7d4bc3d-a55e-5bbb-8cf3-be60cc08dce4" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286107 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534395 true] [17592186286107 1111 #uuid "7a7d6c0b-89d4-58f6-b838-87a105cabcb9" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286108 1034 "II only" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534395 true] [17592186286108 1111 #uuid "1595331b-914e-55d1-ad79-8a6281f2e79b" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286109 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286110 13194139534395 true] [17592186286109 1089 "q-160624" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286109 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534395 true] [17592186286109 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/c32886c161e347daa9bf6fa7215f8a09abc4334632c33020c31ade6dfc4e7315.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286109 1113 #uuid "cec76be9-565e-5e8f-a9d4-8ee2ab561594" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286109 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/ed1a5e8a05ee0d9994d8ba90c02463fbf96525b1cd851bec685a005a34834f81.png)" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286111 13194139534395 true] [17592186286110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286112 13194139534395 true] [17592186286110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286113 13194139534395 true] [17592186286110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286114 13194139534395 true] [17592186286110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286115 13194139534395 true] [17592186286110 1040 #edb/ref 17