739" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286116 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286117 13194139534395 true] [17592186286116 1089 "q-160647" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286116 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534395 true] [17592186286116 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,-5)$\n- $(0,2)$\n- $(-1,-2)$" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286116 1113 #uuid "5ca0ac68-14b1-589e-965f-d2a01a29fa17" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286116 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534395 true] [17592186286117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286118 13194139534395 true] [17592186286117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286119 13194139534395 true] [17592186286117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286120 13194139534395 true] [17592186286117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286121 13194139534395 true] [17592186286117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286122 13194139534395 true] [17592186286117 1040 #edb/ref 1759218604